## Chapter 3 Data Analysis

For some heavy-ion collision with orbital angular momentum direction \(\hat{L}\) the \(\Lambda\) hyperon polarization relative to that angular momentum can be determined by the distribution of the \(\Lambda\)s decay products. For the decay \(\Lambda\to p+\pi^{-}\) the angular distribution of protons in the parent \(\Lambda\) rest frame is given by

\[\frac{dN}{d\cos\theta^{*}}=\frac{1}{2}\left(1+\alpha_{H}|P_{H}|\cos\theta^{*}\right) \tag{3.1}\]

Where \(P_{H}\) is the average polarization of the \(\Lambda\)s, \(\alpha_{\text{H}}\) is the decay parameter of the \(\Lambda\), and \(\theta^{*}\) is the angle between the proton momentum in the mother \(\Lambda\) rest frame and \(\hat{L}\). The '\(H\)' in the subscripts denotes hyperon. For an anti-hyperon, e.g. the \(\overline{\Lambda}\), the script is delineated by an overline. From the particle data group \(\alpha_{\Lambda}\) = -\(\alpha_{\overline{\Lambda}}\) = 0.642\(\pm\)0.013 [43], which is discussed in more detail in sec. A.3. Statistically the daughter proton is emitted in the parent \(\Lambda\) (\(\overline{\Lambda}\)) frame in (opposite) the direction of the \(\Lambda\) (\(\overline{\Lambda}\)) spin. The polarization may depend on \(\Lambda\) emission angle and pseudorapidity. What is reported here is an acceptance integrated measurement. There are a few simplifications that can be made to the description due to the STAR acceptance, which is even in rapidity and azimuthal angle. the coordinate system is shown in fig. 3.2.

By integrating over solid angle it is possible to express the global polarization in terms of the variables available to the experiment

\[P_{H}=\frac{3}{\alpha_{H}}\langle\cos\theta^{*}\rangle \tag{3.2}\]

Where angled brackets denote an the average over events and \(\Lambda\)s for all events. \(\cos\theta^{*}=\sin\theta^{*}\sin\left(\Psi_{\text{RP}}-\phi^{*}\right)\).

