#### 3.5.2 Consequence of helicity efficiency

For the figures in sec. 3.5.1 if we assume that there is no net-polarization signal all of those plots of polarization as a function of \(\phi_{\Lambda}\) will average to zero, as any sine or cosine will over that range. If there actually is a polarization signal then there are some modifications which must be made to that conclusion. The first exception I'll point out is that the polarization could have a dependence on \(\phi_{\Lambda}\). As long as that dependence is second order it will still cancel. We haven't seen any evidence of any second order signal, which likely means the signal itself is just very small. Higher order \(\phi_{\Lambda}\) could potentially exist, but we will assume that no such signal exists. This section will investigate a different affect assuming some nonzero polarization.

