Here, \(f_{\Lambda R}\) (\(f_{\overline{\Lambda R}}\)) is the fraction of measured \(\Lambda\)s (\(\overline{\Lambda}\)s) that arise from the direct decay of a baryon \(R\to\Lambda+X\) (\(\overline{R}\to\overline{\Lambda}+X\)). Similarly, \(f_{\Sigma^{0}R}\) (\(f_{\overline{\Sigma^{0}R}}\)) is the fraction of measured \(\Lambda\)s (\(\overline{\Lambda}\)s) that arise from the direct decay \(R\to\Sigma^{0}+X\to\Lambda+\gamma+X\) (\(\overline{R}\to\overline{\Sigma^{0}}+X\to\overline{\Lambda}+\gamma+X\)). (Note carefully the last sentence: \(f_{\Sigma^{0}R}\) does not give the fraction of _Sigmas_ coming from parent \(R\), but the fraction of _Lambda_ coming from (grand)parent \(R\).) The branching ratio for \(\Sigma^{0}\to\Lambda+\gamma\) is essentially 100%.

